From effd08ded84c98a3779e62a21ec8fdce35b3a9a7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dev <dev@local>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 13:53:09 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] mt7621: model the 1004Kc L1 data cache

The model had no data cache, so KSEG0 and KSEG1 both hit the same RAM.  On
silicon they do not: a cached store lands in the L1D and reaches DRAM through
the next level, while an uncached store (KSEG1, the DMA aliases) bypasses the
cache and leaves any cached copy of that line stale.  Breed depends on that
difference: its DMA wrapper clears a rounded-up pointer through the uncached
alias and runs a few bytes into the next allocator chunk header; the stale
cached copy is what keeps the following malloc working.

- memattrs: add MemTxAttrs.dcache_bypass.
- target/mips: report the mapping's C field (SegmentControl for unmapped
  segments, the TLB entry for mapped pages), mark uncached accesses, and force
  cacheable data pages through the TCG slow path so a machine cache can serve
  them.  synci writes the line back and invalidates it.
- accel/tcg: an optional cpu_dcache_access_handler hook, called from the load
  and store slow paths.
- include/exec: the two hook headers.
